{"vid":"OGU7BeJ5bEs","source":"OGU7BeJ5bEs.en-en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":1.9,"txt":"Business Skills 360, the podcast that looks at the other side of English."},{"t":11.1,"txt":"Hello and welcome back to the Skills 360 podcast. I'm your host, Tim Simmons, and today I want"},{"t":21.4,"txt":"to finish up our series on English interview skills."},{"t":27.6,"txt":"In previous lessons, we've gone over some of the fundamental questions about who you"},{"t":33.4,"txt":"are and what you've done. Today I want to take a closer look at talking about your career"},{"t":41.4,"txt":"goals and researching prospective employers. Interviewers don't just want to know whether"},{"t":49,"txt":"you've got the skills, personality, and qualifications. They also want to know that you're a good"},{"t":56.8,"txt":"fit, and that they are a good fit for you."},{"t":63,"txt":"One important question you need to be ready for in an interview in English is \"why are"},{"t":69.8,"txt":"you leaving your current position?\" This question makes a lot of people squirm. But it's actually"},{"t":76.9,"txt":"an opportunity to talk about growth and change. Nobody stays at the same job their entire"},{"t":85.3,"txt":"life. And this question isn't necessarily fishing for problems in your past."},{"t":94.1,"txt":"What kinds of things can you talk about as reasons for change? You might mention several"},{"t":101.1,"txt":"desires, such as the desire to learn, to take on more responsibility, to enhance certain"},{"t":109.8,"txt":"skills, to relocate, to adjust your work-life balance. All these are great reasons to change."},{"t":119.5,"txt":"Of course, if you were laid off, you need to be candid about that. I'm not suggesting"},{"t":126,"txt":"you lie. Just that you keep your answer future-focused. And that means not badmouthing your current"},{"t":134.6,"txt":"boss, colleagues, or workplace."},{"t":138.8,"txt":"Talking about why you're leaving your current job will segue naturally to answering questions"},{"t":145.2,"txt":"about why you want this job. And the key here is showing knowledge, suitability, and value."},{"t":155,"txt":"By knowledge, I mean you know something about the company, its products, its reputation,"},{"t":162.6,"txt":"its culture, and its values. By suitability, I mean that you jibe with the company and"},{"t":171,"txt":"the job fits with your career goals. And by value, I mean that your skills and experience"},{"t":179.4,"txt":"will help you make a real contribution."},{"t":183.7,"txt":"Speaking of career goals, another common but tough question during a job interview is \"where"},{"t":191.7,"txt":"do you see yourself in five years?\" With most interview questions, I advise people to be"},{"t":198.8,"txt":"specific. But not this one. It's okay to state very generally what your aspirations are."},{"t":206.9,"txt":"For example, you might say \"I'd really like to be involved with the strategic side of"},{"t":212.8,"txt":"things\" or \"I'd like to be taking on more management responsibilities.\""},{"t":220.6,"txt":"Whatever your answer, it should be possible to achieve within the company where you're"},{"t":227,"txt":"applying. They're going to sink time, money, and resources into you. They want to know"},{"t":235.3,"txt":"it will pay off for them, not just you. So, perhaps the worst thing you can say is \"I"},{"t":243.4,"txt":"want to run my own business.\" The interviewer will immediately think you're going to run"},{"t":249.7,"txt":"off with all their ideas!"},{"t":253.5,"txt":"One thing you'll notice is that most of my suggestions require you to actually know something"},{"t":260.1,"txt":"about the company where you're applying. All successful interviewees have done their homework"},{"t":267.3,"txt":"in this regard. You can start with the company website, of course, but also check Google"},{"t":275.3,"txt":"for recent news stories and press releases. Use LinkedIn to find out more about the company's"},{"t":282.5,"txt":"leaders and employees. And look through other social media channels to get a more complete"},{"t":289.4,"txt":"understanding. The Glassdoor website can also give you an inside look at company culture"},{"t":296.7,"txt":"- but don't get scared off by a few negative reviews. Disgruntled people are often the"},{"t":303.9,"txt":"most vocal."},{"t":306.6,"txt":"Researching the company will also help generate some good questions that you can ask. It's"},{"t":312.8,"txt":"fairly standard at or near the end of a job interview for the interviewer to turn the"},{"t":318.8,"txt":"tables with a question like \"Do you have any questions for me?\" The worst thing you can"},{"t":325.3,"txt":"do is to say something like \"no, all good thanks.\" That shows a troubling lack of interest."},{"t":333.7,"txt":"So, what should you ask about? Here's a few good ones. \"How will you measure the success"},{"t":341.3,"txt":"of the person you hire?\" \"What are some of the challenges the person in this position"},{"t":346.7,"txt":"will face?\" \"Can you describe a typical day or week in the job?\" \"How would you describe"},{"t":354.1,"txt":"the culture here?\" And probably one of the best questions, because it gets to the heart"},{"t":360.9,"txt":"of what they're looking for, is \"In the past, what has differentiated the people who were"},{"t":367.5,"txt":"good at this job and those who were great?\""},{"t":372.6,"txt":"That final question will impress the interviewer and give you a good idea if you really want"},{"t":379.3,"txt":"the position or not. After all, unless you're really desperate, an interview is a two-way"},{"t":386.6,"txt":"street. You're both assessing whether you and the job are a good match."},{"t":395.1,"txt":"And as I've explained, you'll have to do that by talking about why you are leaving your"},{"t":401.3,"txt":"current position, why you want this job, and where you see yourself in the future. And"},{"t":409.5,"txt":"through it all, a solid understanding of the company is just as important as an understanding"},{"t":417.5,"txt":"of English for job interviews."},{"t":421.6,"txt":"That's all for today."},{"t":424.6,"txt":"Copyright Business English Pod Ltd. All rights reserved. https://www.businessenglishpod.com"},{"t":439.4,"txt":"So long, stay safe. And happy learning!"}],"words":[{"t":1.92,"w":"Business"},{"t":1.92,"w":"Skills"},{"t":1.92,"w":"360,"},{"t":1.92,"w":"the"},{"t":1.92,"w":"podcast"},{"t":1.92,"w":"that"},{"t":1.92,"w":"looks"},{"t":1.92,"w":"at"},{"t":1.92,"w":"the"},{"t":1.92,"w":"other"},{"t":1.92,"w":"side"},{"t":1.92,"w":"of"},{"t":1.92,"w":"English."},{"t":11.12,"w":"Hello"},{"t":11.12,"w":"and"},{"t":11.12,"w":"welcome"},{"t":11.12,"w":"back"},{"t":11.12,"w":"to"},{"t":11.12,"w":"the"},{"t":11.12,"w":"Skills"},{"t":11.12,"w":"360"},{"t":11.12,"w":"podcast."},{"t":11.12,"w":"I'm"},{"t":11.12,"w":"your"},{"t":11.12,"w":"host,"},{"t":11.12,"w":"Tim"},{"t":11.12,"w":"Simmons,"},{"t":11.12,"w":"and"},{"t":11.12,"w":"today"},{"t":11.12,"w":"I"},{"t":11.12,"w":"want"},{"t":21.45,"w":"to"},{"t":21.45,"w":"finish"},{"t":21.45,"w":"up"},{"t":21.45,"w":"our"},{"t":21.45,"w":"series"},{"t":21.45,"w":"on"},{"t":21.45,"w":"English"},{"t":21.45,"w":"interview"},{"t":21.45,"w":"skills."},{"t":27.56,"w":"In"},{"t":27.56,"w":"previous"},{"t":27.56,"w":"lessons,"},{"t":27.56,"w":"we've"},{"t":27.56,"w":"gone"},{"t":27.56,"w":"over"},{"t":27.56,"w":"some"},{"t":27.56,"w":"of"},{"t":27.56,"w":"the"},{"t":27.56,"w":"fundamental"},{"t":27.56,"w":"questions"},{"t":27.56,"w":"about"},{"t":27.56,"w":"who"},{"t":27.56,"w":"you"},{"t":33.35,"w":"are"},{"t":33.35,"w":"and"},{"t":33.35,"w":"what"},{"t":33.35,"w":"you've"},{"t":33.35,"w":"done."},{"t":33.35,"w":"Today"},{"t":33.35,"w":"I"},{"t":33.35,"w":"want"},{"t":33.35,"w":"to"},{"t":33.35,"w":"take"},{"t":33.35,"w":"a"},{"t":33.35,"w":"closer"},{"t":33.35,"w":"look"},{"t":33.35,"w":"at"},{"t":33.35,"w":"talking"},{"t":33.35,"w":"about"},{"t":33.35,"w":"your"},{"t":33.35,"w":"career"},{"t":41.399,"w":"goals"},{"t